\f1\fs22 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Precisely at that same moment, Sarah felt what she thought to be mild abdominal cramps. The contractions were not painful, rather a noticeable inconvenience accompanied by a disconcerting anxiety radiating from the child. She had channeled his feelings for month and had felt them coalesce from blurred recollections of primordial instinct into identifiable and unique emotion of growing individuality. Mostly he broadcast love and pure joy, which caused her to wonder whether every mother experienced this same connection. More than once, in fact on every occasion, she had concluded her link to her child was special. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The message she was receiving was not coherent, but a transmission of pure thought and emotion absent of language or formal design. It was both beautiful and frightening. She was then blasted with an image of a face, blurred, but familiar. As the image gained clarity, she saw a vision of Johnny, in his lab and the accompanying emotions were of contempt and fear. 
\f2\i Was this link with Richard, she thought, a reanimation of ancient, long forgotten instincts? Was it evolution on steroids? 
\f1\i0 It did not really matter why it had manifested, to her the communication seemed perfectly natural.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The connection Sarah felt with her precious Richard was far more profound than any other mother had ever felt. It was a link unbreakable, Sarah knew it the moment she looked upon him. Losing herself in his eyes set off a deluge of endorphins and waves of joy before unknown to Sarah or, she had concluded, any previous mother.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Her delight had periodically been interrupted by images of Johnny appearing in her mind, accompanied now, with feelings of sorrow and impending doom. Kathy had told her she would experience some fluctuating emotions, but there was no one mentioned strange images of Johnny tinkering away in his lab or feeling like she was mourning the dead. Sarah had dismissed the thought of post natal depression. 
\f2\i Not me! 
\f1\i0 Compared to the extreme unlikelihood that she would fall victim to something as commonplace as a chemical imbalance, the possibility that Richard was the source of the imagery was now compelling. Although, Sarah had to admit, Richard had never even seen Johnny and could not know what he looked like. What it meant, if anything, Sarah could only guess.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93During the pregnancy I felt he was sending messages, just raw feelings, unyoked thoughts and images. Like the image of the beta-mode indicator pattern. The image wasn\'92t just something that I made up in my sleep, but it was real. Since the birth it has become much more noticeable. He has been sending me, at least I think it is him, images of Johnny in his lab. The first time I saw these images was just before I went into labour. I don\'92t know what it means, but afterwards I always feel terrible. Like someone has died.\'94 Richard beamed happiness to Sarah. She was finally getting the message.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Both of those images are linked to Johnny. Which is both interesting and timely. I have received a couple of disturbing reports from the techs working with your arrogant friend.\'94 Hayley disliked Johnny as much as she despised Cracker. She could not work out why Sarah tolerated either of them, or Shakti for that matter. 
\f2\i What is she? An idiot magnet? Then again, isn\'92t every woman? It is our lot to endure oafs of every variety. 
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Interesting? What is he up to?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Members of his team report that he tried to kill them, but worse, far worse, he is planning on testing those blasted discs under centrifugal force.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He\'92s going to spin the discs again?\'94 Sarah was hit with a blast of dread from her Richard who began to cry Hayley\'92s arms. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I think you are right.\'94 Hayley said. \'93Richard understands us.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93If Johnny continues this course of action, his contract will be terminated, with extreme prejudice.\'94 Hayley was firm. Johnny was planning to disobey orders, her orders no less.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I don\'92t see the need for that.\'94 Sarah countered. \'93I am happy to speak with him if you like.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, it will have to be you.\'94 Hayley was pleased. ***\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There was only one bunker in the facility, a large excavation dug originally for a subway station and later earmarked as a bomb shelter for a nuclear conflict that never came. The control room and labs were situated close by, but sufficiently distant, from the bunker to be safely outside any blast zone. With only one suitable location, he was compelled to test both discs in close proximity, a fundamental breach of agreed upon protocols, but necessary, to properly complete the task at hand. So he believed. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny had had several purpose built centrifuges manufactured for this eventuality, which he would have his techs set up in the bunker. The bunker was about 30 metres below ground level, built of concrete many metres thick and after his team had cleared it out completely, was cavernous. The ceiling was 10 metres thick, comprising of heavily reinforced concrete and the walls were lined from floor to ceiling with sandbags in the hope they would absorb the enormous discharges of energy that, no doubt, would be released. Johnny thought, momentarily, to contact his handlers in the police department, but envisaged some dumb-fuck public servant would try to tell him what to do. The very idea! 
\f2\i A uneducated dullard issuing me instructions! Never!
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He decided that keeping the public servants out of the loop was the wisest course of action, given their limited ability to understand complex scientific concepts. No explosions for him. No Way. Not a chance.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>***\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The following morning, just before sun up, Sarah took Richard, Kathy and Cracker took an Elvi piloted \cf3 hover-copt\cf2  to the laboratory complex they were hoping to save from destruction. Upon landing they were greeted by a lab-coat who was desperate to leave as soon as possible and did not mind who he told.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That Johnny idiot is going to kill us all! He tried to irradiated us. It was only pure luck I managed to get out of the lab in time. He is reckless, far too reckless, to be a serious scientist.\'94 Johnny\'92s assistant was glad to see someone had taken heed of his warnings.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny was found alone in the control room, calibrating and re-calibrating the array of instruments and scanners wired to the two centrifuges. He knew he was just procrastinating by doing the busy work that his team of geeks had already completed. They had done a great job, but he resolved not to tell them, just in case it all turned to shit and he needed to point the finger.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Johnny!\'94 Sarah called out. \'93How is the progress?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Turn it off, Johnny.\'94 Sarah was loud and serious. \'93I am not kidding. Switch it off.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It has to cycle through, there is no turning it off.\'94 Johnny was terrified, but unrepentant. His idiotic pride, again had gotten the better of his reason and he had gambled his future.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the discs spun at increasing speed, slender light blue tendrils arced between the discs and became darker and thicker. Sparks were now shooting in all directions, smashing the roof and exploding sand bags and setting the crystals on fire. Sandbags, hundreds of kilograms in weight flew around the room like autumn leaves in a windstorm, spinning and slapping into one another before crashing into the gap between the discs and disappearing into the ether. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Gravity well!\'94 Cried out one of the techs. \'93Holy shit, you can feel it from here.\'94 He threw a notebook vertically into the air, but fell at a 45 degree angle to the ground and slid across the floor, slamming into the wall.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We have to stop this.\'94 Dr Logan said. \'93There must be some way. There must be.\'94 She gave Richard to Cracker and moved closer to the console where Sarah was already working hard, desperately pressing buttons and entering instructions. Johnny was in the foetal position in the corner, watching his career spiral out of control, again. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I am trying, but the security on this system is ridiculous.\'94 Sarah was having no luck. The shooting sparks were now lightening and cracking loudly, desperate to bust out of the bunker, some 100 metres away and 30 metres below. \'93I need more time, a lot more time to crack this.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Suddenly the system was silent and the gravity well disappeared. The panicked crew looked about the control room in amazement, only to see Cracker holding a thick power cable, recently pulled out of a wall, in one hand and Richard-Dawkins in the other. \'93Don\'92t look at me, it was Richard\'92s idea. I looked in his eyes and the next thing I was out the cable.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well,\'94 Cracker commenced. \'93The discs are not opposite are they? If my memory serves me correctly, one is infected with the Hyrbrid-T virus and one is not.\'94 
\f2\i These elites continue to disappoint, he thought.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Are you suggesting I infect them both with the Hybrid T virus?\'94 Johnny countered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No, not at all. I am suggesting you make the discs opposite. One is infected with the T virus. I would suggest the opposite of that is infecting the second discs with the Hyrbid anti-T virus innoculant. Then try spinning them in opposite directions.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is Richard telling you that?\'94 Dr Kathy asked, as Richard began to laugh and point at Cracker. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I do have ideas once in a while.\'94 Cracker said\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We will have to get back to the city, Johnny. Can you promise you will do nothing more until we we come back?\'94 Sarah asked nicely. She did not want to end him, but knew that was a possibility. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Okay, no more experiments for a while. We will have to clean up the bunker and take sample data, do a bit of analysis. That should take a couple of months of study. Shall we meet next week?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93One week from now.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>***\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At that moment every stasis cupboard in the Primary Trade Dominion malfunctioned. Food instantaneously spoiled and turned to dust. At Food and Drug Administration Headquarters, urgent meetings at the highest level were convened. For the first time in it\'92s history something had seriously gone wrong with stasis technology. Sure, a few cupboards had blown fuses and had other minor issues over the years, but never such a catastrophe. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I don\'92t believe this is some sort of accident or natural event,\'94 opined Halverson Tentries III, L.5. Maintenance Technician. \'93Someone is fucking with us.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They clearly don\'92t understand the technology and could do some real damage.\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That is an understatement. We are talking about the end of time.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Listen to Mr Worse-case-Scenario\'94 nbnbnbn \'93It would take some seriously reckless action to mess with space-time.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We can\'92t assume no danger and hope for the best.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just as long as they are not dumb enough to centrifuge the stasis circuitry.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No-one would be that moronic.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You know we will have to examine every stasis cupboard, bin and pantry in the PTD, don\'92t you?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yep.\'94\

